      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The late 15th-century painting prioritizes Renaissance humanism, showcasing intellectual complexity through rigorous adherence to scientific principles like linear perspective and detailed anatomical study. Conversely, the Sub-Saharan masks and textiles demonstrate a profound aesthetic complexity rooted in cosmology, where symbolic patterns and potent visual language serve essential ritual functions. While the painting seeks external, perspectival realism, the African artifacts achieve deep complexity through internal, spiritual meaning embedded in their abstract forms.",
      "source": "target",
